I have everything needed. Here are the raw findings.

---

## (a) Static file serving — NO disk access; traversal is structurally impossible

**There is no request-path-to-filesystem mapping anywhere in this server.** Zero occurrences of `tower_http::fs::ServeDir`, `ServeFile`, `tokio::fs::read`, or `File::open` in any request handler.

Confirmed repo-wide (all `engine/crates/**`):
- `tower-http` is declared with only `features = ["cors", "trace", "limit", "decompression-gzip"]` at `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/Cargo.toml:49` — the `fs` feature is **not** enabled, so `ServeDir`/`ServeFile` are not even compiled into the tree.
- `grep -rn "nest(\|nest_service\|fallback_service\|ServeDir"` across all engine crates returns **no** router hits. There is no `nest`, no `fallback_service`, no fallback route.

### The only file-serving route: embedded asset table

`/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/spa.rs:64`
```rust
include!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/xerj_console_assets.rs"));
```

`/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/spa.rs:80-82`
```rust
fn find_asset(path: &str) -> Option<&'static (&'static str, &'static [u8], &'static str)> {
    XERJ_CONSOLE_ASSETS.iter().find(|(p, _, _)| *p == path)
}
```

The table is generated at build time by `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/build.rs:143-152`, each entry being `("url_path", include_bytes!(r"<abs src path>"), "content_type")`. Serving is an exact string match against a compile-time constant slice — the URL never becomes a filesystem path.

### Path flow (verbatim)

Route: `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/router.rs:50`
```rust
.route("/_xerj-console/*rest", get(spa::xerj_console_asset))
```

Handler: `spa.rs:124-132`
```rust
pub async fn xerj_console_asset(Path(rest): Path<String>) -> impl IntoResponse {
    // Empty rest path = same as `/_xerj-console/` — serve index.
    let path = if rest.is_empty() {
        "index.html"
    } else {
        resolve_request_path(&rest)
    };
    serve(path, false)
}
```

`spa.rs:111-116`
```rust
fn resolve_request_path(rest: &str) -> &str {
    match rest {
        "share" | "share/" => "share/index.html",
        other => other,
    }
}
```

`spa.rs:140-146` — the traversal guard:
```rust
fn serve(path: &str, no_cache: bool) -> Response {
    // Defence-in-depth: no .. components allowed in a relative URL,
    // even though the asset table is statically built and cannot
    // contain them.
    if path.split('/').any(|seg| seg == ".." || seg.is_empty()) {
        return (StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST, "invalid path").into_response();
    }
```

**No `join`, no `canonicalize`, no prefix check — none are needed.** `find_asset` is an equality lookup.

### Answering your two concrete probes

**`GET /%2e%2e/%2e%2e/etc/passwd` (percent-encoded):**
- Percent-decoding happens **after routing**, inside axum's `Path` extractor (axum 0.7, `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/Cargo.toml:34`). Hyper delivers the raw undecoded URI to the matchit router; `Path<String>` then percent-decodes the wildcard capture.
- So `rest == "../../etc/passwd"` at `spa.rs:124`. `serve()` line 144 finds `..` segments → **400**. Even if the guard were absent, `find_asset` would return `None` → **404**. No disk read occurs in either case.

**`GET /../../etc/passwd` (raw dots):**
- matchit does not normalize `..`. Note the `/_xerj-console` prefix must be consumed first, so a raw-dots payload would have to be `/_xerj-console/../../etc/passwd` → `rest = "../../etc/passwd"` → **400** at line 144. Same conclusion.
- `GET /../../etc/passwd` *without* the prefix does not match any route on either router and falls through to axum's default empty-router 404. There is no catch-all handler to land in.

**One behavioral note (not a vuln):** the same guard also rejects *empty* segments, so `/_xerj-console//share.js` and `/_xerj-console/share//share.js` return **400** rather than 404. Pinned at `spa.rs:397`.

**Extensionless fallback** — `spa.rs:156-161` appends `.html` only when the last segment contains no `.`, then re-runs `find_asset`. It never opens a file, so it cannot be used to reach arbitrary names.

---

## (b) Console router merge — mounted at root via `Router::merge`; engine auth does NOT cover it

`/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/main.rs:2765-2771`
```rust
    let mut native_router = build_native_router(state.clone());
    let mut es_router = build_es_compat_router(state.clone());
    if let Some(console_state) = xerj_console_state {
        let xerj_console_router = xerj_console_api::xerj_console_router(console_state);
        native_router = native_router.merge(xerj_console_router.clone());
        es_router = es_router.merge(xerj_console_router);
    }
```

Facts, precisely:
- **Mounted at the root** (not `nest`ed, not a `fallback`). The console router owns `/_xerj-console/*` internally — `xerj-console-api/src/router.rs:45-50`.
- **Merged into BOTH listeners** — `:8080` native *and* `:9200` ES-compat. `serve_on` at `main.rs:1127-1189` applies no further layers: it goes straight to `router.into_make_service_with_connect_info::<SocketAddr>()` (line 1160 rustls path, line 1180 plain path).
- **Merge happens after all `.layer()` calls.** Both engine routers finish their middleware stacks inside their builders: `xerj-api/src/router.rs:185-215` (native) and `:960-998` (ES-compat). The auth layer is applied at `xerj-api/src/router.rs:200` (native) and `:975-978` (ES-compat) — before `main.rs:2769-2770` merges.
- In axum 0.7, `.layer()` wraps only routes registered before the call, and `Router::merge` does not propagate one router's layers onto the other's routes. **Therefore the engine `auth_middleware` (Bearer/API-key) never runs on `/_xerj-console/*`, and conversely the engine routes never acquire the console's session-cookie auth.** Neither direction of accidental coverage occurs.
- This is asserted in-repo at `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-api/src/auth.rs:217-220`:
```rust
    // Xerj Console lives in a peer router (mounted by `xerj-server` via
    // `Router::merge`) and runs its own session-cookie auth, so this
    // middleware never sees `/_xerj-console/*` requests — no path-prefix
    // bypass needed.
```
  and `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/tests/console_cannot_read_a_brain.rs:5-7`:
```rust
//! `xerj_api::authz` middleware, but the Console router is merged onto the
//! engine routers *after* their layers are applied (`xerj-server::main`), so
//! that middleware never runs on `/_xerj-console/*` and no index-visibility
```
- No `fallback_layer` exists anywhere. There is **no** `nest`, so no `/_xerj-console` prefix-stripping bug is possible.

**Corollary worth flagging (design consequence, not a bug):** because authz is layer-based on the engine and the console is merged past it, the console's in-process engine calls run with *no* authorization scope installed. That was a realRC10 blocker, fixed by refusing the reserved namespace inside `data_sources.rs` itself — see `console_cannot_read_a_brain.rs:1-13` and the `_sess: AuthSession` + explicit `.xerj_*`/`.xerj-memory-*` refusals at `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/data_sources.rs:79-81, 123-126, 177-180, 246-249`.

**Console auth posture** — there is no middleware at all on the console router (last builder call is `.with_state(state)` at `router.rs:173`). Auth is per-handler via extractor:
- `AuthSession` is fail-closed: `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/auth/sessions.rs:136-164` rejects with `Unauthorized("no session")` / `("bad session signature")` / `("unknown session")` / `("session revoked")`. Bearer wins over cookie when both are present (lines 147-149).
- The **only** handler with no session extractor is `cluster::info` at `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-console-api/src/cluster.rs:23` — deliberate and documented as AUTHZ-2 at lines 34-42, with a test pinning that `node_id`/`version` are absent from the pre-auth body (`cluster.rs:55-68`).
- `OptionalAuthSession` is defined at `sessions.rs:276-291` but has **zero call sites** outside its own definition — no handler currently uses the fail-open variant.

---

## (c) Other HTML / file-serving routes

**None in `xerj-server/src/`.** Grep for `text/html`, `Html`, `include_str`, `include_bytes`, `Content-Disposition`, `Attachment` across `main.rs`, `share.rs`, `brain.rs`, `grpc.rs`, `debug_profiling.rs`, `ingest_memory_trace.rs` returns only comments and unrelated identifiers (`merge`/`fallback` hits are query-planner text and shutdown comments).

The only `text/html` producer in the whole engine is the console SPA (`xerj-console-api/src/spa.rs:311` test assertion; content types come from `build.rs:161 content_type_for`).

**Non-request-driven disk I/O in `xerj-server/src/` (all safe — CLI/startup, never a request path):**
- `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/main.rs:1373` — `std::fs::File::open(&cmd.file)` — the `xerj ingest` subcommand's NDJSON input, sourced from CLI args.
- `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/main.rs:894-899` — `key_path.canonicalize().zip(autogen.canonicalize().ok())` — startup TLS key permission tightening, comparing two canonical paths. Not reachable from any request.
- `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/main.rs:825` — `std::fs::read_to_string(&key_path)` — master-key load at boot.
- `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/main.rs:3244,3257` and `debug_profiling.rs:325,361,383`, `ingest_memory_trace.rs:389` — all inside `#[cfg(test)]` modules.
- `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-server/src/share.rs:678`, `brain.rs:266,696,756,771` — CLI/config paths, not HTTP handlers.

**Hardening already present on the console HTML responses** (relevant context, no action needed): `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` on every asset (`spa.rs:191`, `spa.rs:226`); `CONSOLE_CSP` with `default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'` (`spa.rs:76`) on `index.html`; `GUEST_CSP` plus `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `no-store`, COOP/CORP (`spa.rs:99-101`, `spa.rs:218-248`) on the unauthenticated guest page. Guest-asset classification is prefix-based on the *asset table* path, not the request (`spa.rs:104-107`), so `serve("share/index", false)` still gets the guest header set.

---

## (d) CORS — `allow_origin(Any)` exists but is opt-in, and is never combined with credentials

`/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-api/src/router.rs:1110-1149`
```rust
fn cors_layer(cfg: &CorsConfig) -> CorsLayer {
    if cfg.allow_any_origin {
        // Opt-in dev escape hatch — restores the legacy wide-open behavior.
        return CorsLayer::new()
            .allow_origin(Any)
            .allow_methods(Any)
            .allow_headers(Any);
    }
    if cfg.allowed_origins.is_empty() {
        // Restrictive default: no `allow_origin` configured → tower-http emits
        // no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, so cross-origin browser reads fail.
        return CorsLayer::new();
    }
    ...
    CorsLayer::new()
        .allow_origin(AllowOrigin::list(origins))
        .allow_methods([...])
        .allow_headers(Any)
}
```

- **`grep -rn "allow_credentials"` across the entire `engine/` tree returns zero results.** No CORS layer anywhere sets `allow_credentials(true)`. The `Any` + credentials combination you asked about does **not** occur. Even in the `allow_any_origin` escape hatch, `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` is never emitted, so browsers refuse credentialed cross-origin reads.
- Default posture is restrictive: `allowed_origins` empty + `allow_any_origin = false` → bare `CorsLayer::new()`, i.e. no CORS headers at all. Defaults at `/root/ab-run-p1/repo/engine/crates/xerj-common/src/config.rs:713-728`.
- `allow_origin(Any)` requires an explicit `cors.allow_any_origin = true`; the doc comment at `router.rs:1096-1109` records that the pre-RC4 layer hard-coded this and it was made opt-in.
- CORS is applied innermost of the middleware stack (`router.rs:203` native, `router.rs:986` ES-compat), i.e. applied last = runs first, before auth. That is correct for CORS preflight.
- **Note:** `allow_headers(Any)` is used in *both* the wide-open and the allow-listed branch (`router.rs:1116` and `router.rs:1148`). With `allow_credentials` unset this is not exploitable, but it does mean an allow-listed origin can send any header. Minor.
- **The console routes have no CORS layer at all.** `xerj_console_router` (`xerj-console-api/src/router.rs:45-174`) contains no `.layer(...)`, and `xerj-console-api/src/` has zero `CorsLayer` references. Since the merge happens after `cors` is applied, `/_xerj-console/api/v1/*` emits no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` on either port. For a cookie/bearer-authenticated same-origin SPA this is the safe direction (fail-closed), but it also means the engine's `cors.allowed_origins` configuration silently does not apply to the console API — an operator tuning CORS would see it take effect on the data plane and not the console. Worth an operator-facing note.
